{"id": "2026-10-13-news-eu-adopts-record-russia-sanctions-listings", "date": "2026-10-13", "stream": "news", "headline": "EU adopts record Russia sanctions listings", "summary": "EU foreign ministers finalised the largest-ever expansion of Russia sanctions listings on 12 October, about 1,600 entries plus 77 over occupied-territory Duma elections.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/germany-s-wadephul-calls-new-eu-sanctions-unprecedented-blow-to-russia/a-79638017", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "EU foreign ministers meeting in Luxembourg on Monday 12 October finalised what EU foreign-policy chief Kaja Kallas called \"the single largest expansion of our sanctions list\" since Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine. The package adds about 1,600 individuals and companies linked to Russia's war effort. A further 77 people are listed over what the EU considers illegal Russian Duma elections held in occupied Ukrainian territory. Kallas said more than half of the new listings are tied to the production chain for ballistic missiles. Pre-adoption reporting by European Pravda put the main block at 1,569 listings (743 individuals and 826 entities), agreed by EU ambassadors on Wednesday 7 October. German Foreign Minister Johann Wadephul called it an \"unprecedented sanction blow\". Polish Prime Minister Donald Tusk said the West should go further and that he would push EU partners in Brussels to tighten sanctions again. The figures here come from Kallas and the press, not from the Council's legal acts, which could not be reached this run.", "why": "these listings are asset freezes and travel bans on individuals and companies, not new sector-wide trade bans. They target the supply chain for the missiles Russia fires at Ukrainian cities and power plants as a fifth war winter approaches.", "thread_id": "2026-10-13-news-eu-adopts-record-russia-sanctions-listings", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-13", "event_date": "2026-10-12", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["European Union", "Russia", "Ukraine", "Kaja Kallas"]}
{"id": "2026-10-13-news-drones-sink-ship-off-bulgarian-coast-again", "date": "2026-10-13", "stream": "news", "headline": "Drones sink ship off Bulgarian coast again", "summary": "Drones hit two ships off Bulgaria's Black Sea coast on 13 October, sinking one, a week after a similar attack.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/13/drone-attack-sinks-ship-off-bulgarias-black-sea-coast-pm-says", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Drones hit two ships near Bulgaria's Black Sea coast early on Tuesday 13 October, and one of them sank, Bulgarian Prime Minister Rumen Radev said, a week after a similar attack in the same waters. The Ukrainian-owned, Maltese-flagged Chelsea 3 was struck by three drones about 18 nautical miles (33 km) off the coast at about 01:30 local time. Its 15 crew, seven Ukrainians and eight Azerbaijanis, were rescued, and none was seriously hurt. One crew member was also rescued from the ship that sank. Radev said there was \"no clear evidence\" of who launched the drones, but that \"the outcome benefits\" Russia. He said Russia's ambassador would be summoned and that he would speak to NATO Secretary-General Mark Rutte. European Commission President Ursula von der Leyen condemned the attacks. In the earlier attack on Tuesday 6 October, two ships were hit and one sank, and all 10 crew of the sunken vessel are still missing.", "why": "the war at sea is now hitting merchant shipping next to the territorial waters of an EU and NATO member, and Bulgaria has to answer it without knowing for certain who is responsible.", "thread_id": "2026-10-13-news-drones-sink-ship-off-bulgarian-coast-again", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-13", "event_date": "2026-10-13", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Bulgaria", "Black Sea", "Rumen Radev", "NATO"]}
{"id": "2026-10-13-news-kabila-takes-command-of-m23-backed-front", "date": "2026-10-13", "stream": "news", "headline": "Kabila takes command of M23-backed front", "summary": "Joseph Kabila launched the Front for the Refoundation of the Nation with AFC-M23 in Goma on 12 October.", "url": "https://www.rfi.fr/fr/afrique/20261012-rdc-joseph-kabila-prend-la-t%C3%AAte-du-front-pour-la-refondation-de-la-nation-nouveau-mouvement-politico-miltaire", "source_domain": "rfi.fr", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Former Congolese president Joseph Kabila launched a new political-military coalition with the Rwanda-backed M23 armed group on the evening of Monday 12 October in Goma, the eastern city M23 has held since January 2025. The Front for the Refoundation of the Nation (FRN) brings in the Congo River Alliance (AFC) and M23. Kabila leads it, with AFC-M23 figures Corneille Nangaa, Bertrand Bisimwa and Sultani Makenga among his vice-presidents. A coalition adviser said the aim is to end \"tyranny, by any means necessary including the use of force\". Kinshasa had not responded at the time of reporting. A Congolese military court sentenced Kabila to death in absentia last year for treason, war crimes and crimes against humanity, which he calls politically motivated. The US sanctioned him earlier this year. Government–M23 talks are due in Qatar in November.", "why": "a former head of state now leads the armed rebellion openly. Analysts quoted by Al Jazeera expect a military escalation that could sink the Qatar-mediated peace process.", "thread_id": "2026-10-13-news-kabila-takes-command-of-m23-backed-front", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-13", "event_date": "2026-10-12", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Joseph Kabila", "M23", "DR Congo", "Goma"]}
{"id": "2026-10-13-news-uk-formally-blames-iran-for-fairford-plot", "date": "2026-10-13", "stream": "news", "headline": "UK formally blames Iran for Fairford plot", "summary": "UK PM Burnham said on 12 October the RAF Fairford plot was highly likely coordinated by Iran's IRGC and announced sanctions.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/uk-iran-highly-likely-to-be-behind-raf-fairford-incident/a-79645941", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics", "security"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "UK Prime Minister Andy Burnham told the House of Commons on Monday 12 October that the alleged plot outside RAF Fairford was \"highly likely\" coordinated in Iran and \"almost certainly directed\" by its Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC). Fairford is a UK air base used by US heavy bombers. London summoned Iran's ambassador and sanctioned two individuals, and Burnham said further sanctions could follow. Five young British men arrested near the base on Sunday 27 September were released on bail, and a sixth arrest followed. Preliminary reports say no viable explosives were found in their vans, only accelerants such as petrol, so the scale of the threat remains unclear. Iran's ambassador \"categorically\" rejected the accusation and asked London to present evidence. This firms up the \"strong indications\" Burnham cited earlier this month into a formal attribution.", "why": "Britain is publicly blaming Tehran for organising an attack on its own soil while US bombers that can reach Iran operate from British bases.", "thread_id": "2026-10-13-news-uk-formally-blames-iran-for-fairford-plot", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-13", "event_date": "2026-10-12", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["United Kingdom", "Iran", "IRGC", "RAF Fairford", "Andy Burnham"]}
{"id": "2026-10-13-news-imf-says-resilience-is-reaching-its-limits", "date": "2026-10-13", "stream": "news", "headline": "IMF says resilience is reaching its limits", "summary": "The IMF's October World Economic Outlook nudged 2026 global growth to 3.1% and warned buffers are depleted.", "url": "https://www.imf.org/en/Publications/WEO/Issues/2026/10/13/world-economic-outlook-october-2026", "source_domain": "imf.org", "tier": "T1", "tags": [], "topics": ["economy"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The IMF raised its 2026 global growth forecast slightly, to 3.1% from 3.0% in July, in its World Economic Outlook published on Tuesday 13 October in Bangkok. It also warned that the world economy \"could soon test the limits of resilience\". Its 2027 forecast is unchanged at 3.4%. US growth is projected at 2.3% for both 2026 and 2027. The Fund said growth had withstood the US–Iran war better than feared, because oil was rerouted away from the Strait of Hormuz and strategic stocks were released. It warned that those emergency stocks are now depleted and that high debt leaves many governments little room to protect consumers from another price shock. IMF chief economist Silvana Tenreyro said resilience \"is reaching its limits\".", "why": "the IMF is saying the safety margins that absorbed this year's energy shock have been used up, so another disruption would hit prices faster.", "thread_id": "2026-10-13-news-imf-says-resilience-is-reaching-its-limits", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-13", "event_date": "2026-10-13", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["IMF", "World Economic Outlook"]}
{"id": "2026-10-13-news-hungary-repeals-orb-n-era-anti-lgbtq-curbs", "date": "2026-10-13", "stream": "news", "headline": "Hungary repeals Orbán-era anti-LGBTQ curbs", "summary": "Hungary's parliament voted 139-51 on 13 October to remove anti-LGBTQ+ provisions from the 2021 child protection law.", "url": "https://www.rfi.fr/fr/europe/20261013-la-hongrie-adopte-une-premi%C3%A8re-loi-supprimant-des-dispositions-anti-lgbtq-de-l-%C3%A8re-orban", "source_domain": "rfi.fr", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Hungary's parliament voted 139 to 51 on Tuesday 13 October to strip the anti-LGBTQ+ provisions from a 2021 \"child protection\" law passed under former prime minister Viktor Orbán. The 2021 law banned \"promoting\" or depicting homosexuality and gender change to under-18s, and was later tightened to justify banning Pride marches. It is the first significant rollback of Orbán-era LGBTQ+ restrictions since Péter Magyar took office in May. Magyar says the change brings Hungary into line with EU law, after the 2021 legislation was found contrary to EU values. He presented it as strengthening child protection \"regardless of sexual orientation\" rather than as restoring LGBTQ+ rights.", "why": "it removes one of the main legal disputes between Budapest and Brussels, and shows Magyar is changing course carefully rather than presenting it as a break with Orbán.", "thread_id": "2026-10-13-news-hungary-repeals-orb-n-era-anti-lgbtq-curbs", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-13", "event_date": "2026-10-13", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Hungary", "Péter Magyar", "Viktor Orbán"]}
{"id": "2026-10-13-news-federal-agents-search-ilhan-omar-s-home", "date": "2026-10-13", "stream": "news", "headline": "Federal agents search Ilhan Omar's home", "summary": "Homeland Security Investigations searched Rep. Ilhan Omar's home on 12 October under a warrant; no charges announced.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/us-federal-agents-search-home-of-trump-critic-ilhan-omar/a-79643360", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "US federal agents searched the Washington-area home of Democratic Representative Ilhan Omar on Monday 12 October, three weeks before the midterm elections. The Justice Department said Homeland Security Investigations, the criminal-investigation arm of the US immigration agency ICE, executed a \"judicially authorized search warrant\" as part of an \"ongoing federal investigation\". It did not name Omar or give details, and no charges have been announced. Omar called the search the \"latest escalation\" in President Trump's \"campaign of political intimidation\" and said she \"will not be silenced\". House Democratic leaders called it an \"abuse of power\". Trump has repeatedly attacked Omar, who came to the US from Somalia as a child, and has called Somalis \"garbage\".", "why": "an opposition lawmaker's home was searched just before a national election, in an investigation the government has not described. That makes it hard to judge from the outside whether the case is legitimate or political.", "thread_id": "2026-10-13-news-federal-agents-search-ilhan-omar-s-home", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-13", "event_date": "2026-10-12", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Ilhan Omar", "US Department of Justice", "Donald Trump"]}
{"id": "2026-10-13-news-audit-finds-half-of-pesticide-files-stuck", "date": "2026-10-13", "stream": "news", "headline": "Audit finds half of pesticide files stuck", "summary": "The Swiss Federal Audit Office found half of pesticide approval applications filed 2018-2025 still pending and emergency authorisations exceeding ordinary ones.", "url": "https://www.efk.admin.ch/fr/audit/efficacite-dans-lhomologation-de-produits-phytosanitaires/", "source_domain": "efk.admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The Swiss Federal Audit Office (CDF/EFK) found on Monday 12 October that half of all pesticide approval applications filed between 2018 and 2025 are still pending. Approval is the federal clearance a plant-protection product needs before it can be sold. Le Temps puts the figure at 557 of 1,117 applications, and 81% of those filed in 2024. Since 2018 the Federal Food Safety and Veterinary Office (FSVO/OSAV) has closed fewer files each year than it received, and emergency authorisations have filled the gap: 34 in 2024, against 18 ordinary approvals. The auditors blame a process split across five federal offices in three departments, no binding deadlines, digitisation that is still ineffective, and fees that cover only 15% of costs. The three responsible federal councillors have asked for an action plan to clear the backlog.", "why": "emergency authorisations, granted without the full ordinary review, have become the main way pesticides reach Swiss fields. The auditors want binding deadlines and higher fees so the ordinary review is used again.", "thread_id": "2026-10-13-news-audit-finds-half-of-pesticide-files-stuck", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-13", "event_date": "2026-10-12", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Swiss Federal Audit Office", "FSVO"]}
{"id": "2026-10-13-news-foph-widens-rsv-vaccine-advice-for-winter", "date": "2026-10-13", "stream": "news", "headline": "FOPH widens RSV vaccine advice for winter", "summary": "Switzerland's FOPH issued 2026-27 winter vaccination advice, extending RSV vaccination to high-risk adults from 18.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/UhZ5xgU3Ltn4", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "health"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The Federal Office of Public Health (FOPH) published its winter vaccination recommendations on Tuesday 13 October, widening RSV vaccination and dropping the general COVID-19 recommendation for healthy pregnant women. RSV (respiratory syncytial virus) is the leading cause of hospitalisation in newborns. The RSV vaccine is now also recommended for adults aged 18 and over with serious pre-existing conditions and a very high risk of severe illness, in addition to everyone aged 75 and over and those aged 65 and over with certain conditions. COVID-19 vaccination stays recommended for people aged 65 and over and those with certain conditions or trisomy 21. Flu advice is unchanged: the best time to get vaccinated runs from mid-October until the flu wave, which usually begins in the second half of December. Delivery delays for some flu vaccines are affecting Switzerland and the rest of Europe, but the FOPH says everyone who wants a shot will get one before the wave. National vaccination weeks in surgeries and pharmacies run from Monday 2 to Saturday 7 November and from Monday 23 to Saturday 28 November.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-10-13-news-foph-widens-rsv-vaccine-advice-for-winter", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-13", "event_date": "2026-10-13", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["FOPH", "RSV", "COVID-19"]}
{"id": "2026-10-13-news-tenants-deliver-100-000-signature-rent-appeal", "date": "2026-10-13", "stream": "news", "headline": "Tenants deliver 100,000-signature rent appeal", "summary": "The Swiss tenants' association handed in a 100,848-signature appeal asking the Federal Council to drop its counter-proposal to the rent initiative.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/schweiz/mietpreis-initiative-mieterverband-will-stopp-des-gegenvorschlags", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "The Swiss tenants' association (MV/ASLOCA) handed the Federal Chancellery an appeal with 100,848 signatures, gathered in two weeks, asking the Federal Council to stop drafting its indirect counter-proposal to the rent-price initiative, SRF reported on Tuesday 13 October. The initiative would require rents to be checked and lowered automatically when too high, and would tie them to actual costs. The counter-proposal, which the Federal Council decided on on Friday 25 September, would instead let a starting rent be matched to rents for similar flats nearby, and would allow increases every four years without any review. The association argues that every new, higher rent would feed back into that local comparison, \"fuelling the rent spiral\". Association president and Socialist member of the Council of States Carlo Sommaruga said tenants already pay too much.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-10-13-news-tenants-deliver-100-000-signature-rent-appeal", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-13", "event_date": "2026-10-13", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Swiss Tenants' Association", "Federal Council"]}
